1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,618
Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,618 --> 00:00:18,393
This is a place for slow stories and soft voices, and tonight there is nothing at all you need to do.

3
00:00:18,393 --> 00:00:21,241
You do not have to follow every word.

4
00:00:21,241 --> 00:00:30,402
If your thoughts wander away, let them go, and if sleep arrives before the end, that is exactly what this story is for.

5
00:00:31,902 --> 00:00:32,929
So settle in.

6
00:00:32,929 --> 00:00:35,537
Pull the blanket a little higher.

7
00:00:35,537 --> 00:00:48,734
Let your jaw unclench, and your forehead smooth out, and let one long breath leave you, slowly, like the last thread of smoke rising from a fire that is almost asleep.

8
00:00:50,234 --> 00:01:01,599
Tonight we are travelling north, about a thousand years back in time, to a farm at the head of a Norwegian fjord, in the deepest part of winter.

9
00:01:01,599 --> 00:01:03,651
Snow lies over everything.

10
00:01:03,651 --> 00:01:08,228
The days are short and pale, and the nights are very long.

11
00:01:08,228 --> 00:01:15,173
And at the centre of the farm stands one long, low house, with a fire burning inside it.

12
00:01:16,673 --> 00:01:24,920
We will spend the whole of one winter evening in that house, with the family and the animals and the fire.

13
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:26,865
Nothing much will happen.

14
00:01:26,865 --> 00:01:28,577
And that is the point.

15
00:01:38,077 --> 00:01:43,948
Imagine a long arm of the sea, reaching far inland between steep mountains.

16
00:01:43,948 --> 00:01:55,925
The water is dark and very still, and the mountains rise straight out of it, white with snow, so high that in midwinter the sun barely clears their tops.

17
00:01:55,925 --> 00:01:57,178
This is a fjord.

18
00:01:57,178 --> 00:02:05,554
The western coast of Norway is cut by dozens of them, some running more than a hundred miles into the land.

19
00:02:07,054 --> 00:02:15,393
At the very end of this fjord, where a river comes down from the hills, there is a small patch of flat ground.

20
00:02:15,393 --> 00:02:20,775
In summer it is green, with meadows for hay and a few strips of barley.

21
00:02:20,775 --> 00:02:30,478
Now it is buried under a smooth, deep layer of snow, broken only by a line of footprints leading from the shore up to the house.

22
00:02:31,978 --> 00:02:35,314
A farm like this was never just one building.

23
00:02:35,314 --> 00:02:41,245
Around the long house there are several smaller ones, scattered across the snow.

24
00:02:41,245 --> 00:02:46,731
A storehouse raised up on stones, to keep the food dry and away from mice.

25
00:02:46,731 --> 00:02:52,142
A low workshop half dug into the ground, where the iron tools are mended.

26
00:02:52,142 --> 00:02:53,699
A shed for the sheep.

27
00:02:53,699 --> 00:03:01,632
Each roof is a soft white hump, and each is joined to the others by narrow paths, trodden flat in the snow.

28
00:03:03,132 --> 00:03:06,650
The people who live here are one large household.

29
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:19,717
A farmer and his wife, their children, an old grandmother who has seen more winters than anyone else in the valley, and two or three workers who help with the animals and the fields.

30
00:03:19,717 --> 00:03:27,686
Through the dark months, they will all live, and eat, and sleep together, in the one long room around the fire.

31
00:03:29,186 --> 00:03:36,855
The people who lived here a thousand years ago did not divide the year into four seasons the way we do.

32
00:03:36,855 --> 00:03:41,470
In the old Norse way of reckoning, there were really only two.

33
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:42,885
Summer, and winter.

34
00:03:42,885 --> 00:03:54,872
Winter began in the middle of autumn, with a time called the winter nights, and from then on, for half the year, life drew inward, toward the house and the fire.

35
00:03:56,372 --> 00:04:01,225
Their months had names that tell you a good deal about life in the cold.

36
00:04:01,225 --> 00:04:18,685
In Iceland, where these old names were remembered longest, the month from late October to late November was called Gormánuður, the slaughter month, when animals that could not be fed through the winter were killed, and their meat was salted, smoked, or dried.

37
00:04:18,685 --> 00:04:26,707
The month after that, the month we are in tonight, was called Ýlir, and it led up to the great midwinter feast of Yule.

38
00:04:28,208 --> 00:04:39,147
Down by the shore, a small wooden boat has been pulled high up onto the stones and turned over, so that the snow slides off its curved planks.

39
00:04:39,147 --> 00:04:45,696
Beside it, there is a long boathouse, half sunk into the ground, with a roof of turf.

40
00:04:45,696 --> 00:04:52,784
Inside, the farm's larger boat waits out the winter, its oars stacked neatly along the wall.

41
00:04:54,284 --> 00:05:07,146
The air is so cold and so still that the smoke from the house rises in a straight grey column, without bending at all, until it fades into the darkening sky.

42
00:05:07,146 --> 00:05:17,388
Somewhere across the water, a raven calls once, a deep, hollow sound, and the mountains send it back, softer, a moment later.

43
00:05:18,888 --> 00:05:22,038
It is only the middle of the afternoon.

44
00:05:22,038 --> 00:05:24,542
But already the light is going.

45
00:05:24,542 --> 00:05:30,115
The snow on the high peaks turns faintly pink, then lilac, then grey.

46
00:05:30,115 --> 00:05:37,789
And down below, in the long house at the head of the fjord, someone is adding wood to the fire.

47
00:05:47,289 --> 00:05:51,634
Let us walk up the path through the snow, toward the house.

48
00:05:51,634 --> 00:06:01,503
It is long and low, perhaps twenty paces from end to end, with walls that curve gently outward in the middle, like the side of a boat.

49
00:06:01,503 --> 00:06:12,697
The roof is thick with turf and covered in snow, so that from a distance the whole house looks like a long white hill, with smoke rising from its spine.

50
00:06:14,197 --> 00:06:18,657
The door is small, and you have to stoop a little to go in.

51
00:06:18,657 --> 00:06:23,419
Inside there is a narrow entrance room, and then a second door.

52
00:06:23,419 --> 00:06:27,804
This was a simple and clever way of keeping the warmth in.

53
00:06:27,804 --> 00:06:35,061
When the outer door opened, the cold air stopped in the little room, and never reached the fire.

54
00:06:36,561 --> 00:06:38,343
And then you are inside.

55
00:06:38,343 --> 00:06:41,463
It takes a moment for your eyes to adjust.

56
00:06:41,463 --> 00:06:44,582
There are no windows here, or almost none.

57
00:06:44,582 --> 00:06:56,465
The only light comes from the fire, and from a few small lamps, and from a square hole in the roof high above, where the smoke drifts slowly out into the night.

58
00:06:57,965 --> 00:07:08,827
The house is divided by two long rows of wooden posts, running the length of the room, thick tree trunks holding up the great weight of the roof.

59
00:07:08,827 --> 00:07:12,723
They make three aisles, like the inside of a church.

60
00:07:12,723 --> 00:07:20,514
Down the central aisle, the floor is beaten earth, dry and hard, scattered with ash to soak up the damp.

61
00:07:22,014 --> 00:07:25,720
In the middle of that floor is the long hearth.

62
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:32,027
It is a low, rectangular bed of stones, set into the ground, several paces long.

63
00:07:32,027 --> 00:07:38,807
A fire of birch logs burns along it, not roaring, just steady, glowing orange and red.

64
00:07:38,807 --> 00:07:49,214
Its warmth reaches out to both sides of the house, and its light flickers up along the posts and into the smoky shadows of the roof.

65
00:07:50,714 --> 00:08:01,570
Along both walls, on either side of the fire, there are wide wooden platforms, raised a little off the floor, and running almost the whole length of the house.

66
00:08:01,570 --> 00:08:05,804
These were the benches, and they were the heart of daily life.

67
00:08:05,804 --> 00:08:15,226
People sat on them to eat, and worked on them through the day, and at night, they spread out furs and woollen blankets, and slept on them.

68
00:08:16,726 --> 00:08:28,915
Only the farmer and his wife had a proper bed of their own, in a small closed space at one end of the room, like a wooden cupboard with a door, lined with straw and sheepskins.

69
00:08:28,915 --> 00:08:31,339
Everyone else slept on the benches.

70
00:08:31,339 --> 00:08:34,733
And there was very little other furniture at all.

71
00:08:34,733 --> 00:08:45,398
A few wooden chests stood along the walls, and the low tables used for meals were simply lifted up and hung from the roof beams when they were not needed.

72
00:08:46,898 --> 00:08:52,767
The woman of the house has a bunch of keys hanging from a brooch at her shoulder.

73
00:08:52,767 --> 00:08:56,898
They open the chests, and the storehouse, and the pantry.

74
00:08:56,898 --> 00:09:01,390
In the old Norse world, the keys were a sign of her authority.

75
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:12,477
The house, and everything stored in it to carry the household through the winter, was hers to manage, and she knew to the last handful how much was left.

76
00:09:13,977 --> 00:09:18,363
Here and there along the benches, small lamps are burning.

77
00:09:18,363 --> 00:09:24,943
Some are shallow bowls carved from soapstone, a soft grey stone that was easy to shape.

78
00:09:24,943 --> 00:09:36,288
They are filled with oil made from fish livers, or from seals, and the wick is a little twist of fluffy cotton grass gathered from the bogs in summer.

79
00:09:36,288 --> 00:09:41,582
The flame is small and steady, and gives just enough light to work by.

80
00:09:43,082 --> 00:09:49,671
The air smells of woodsmoke and wool, of drying fish and hay and warm animals.

81
00:09:49,671 --> 00:09:52,543
It is not a perfectly clean smell.

82
00:09:52,543 --> 00:09:55,415
But it is a deeply comforting one.

83
00:09:55,415 --> 00:09:59,808
It is the smell of a house that is ready for winter.

84
00:10:09,307 --> 00:10:16,592
At the far end of the house, beyond a wooden partition, you can hear soft sounds in the dark.

85
00:10:16,592 --> 00:10:18,550
A long, contented breath.

86
00:10:18,550 --> 00:10:21,057
The shifting of hooves on straw.

87
00:10:21,057 --> 00:10:23,720
The slow, steady grinding of jaws.

88
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:35,078
This end of the building is the byre, and in many longhouses of this time, the cattle lived under the same roof as the family through the winter.

89
00:10:36,578 --> 00:10:38,558
There was good sense in it.

90
00:10:38,558 --> 00:10:48,385
The animals were safe from the cold and the wolves, and they were easy to feed and milk without anyone having to go out into the snow.

91
00:10:48,385 --> 00:10:57,698
And their bodies gave off a gentle warmth of their own, which drifted through the house and helped the fire keep out the frost.

92
00:10:59,198 --> 00:11:01,605
There are only a few cows here.

93
00:11:01,605 --> 00:11:08,204
They are small and sturdy, much smaller than modern cattle, with shaggy winter coats.

94
00:11:08,204 --> 00:11:16,124
Each stands in its own narrow stall, divided by stone slabs or wooden boards set upright in the floor.

95
00:11:16,124 --> 00:11:24,819
One of them turns her head as you come near, looks at you calmly with large dark eyes, and goes back to her hay.

96
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:34,040
In the stall nearest the door stands a small horse, with a thick, rough coat and a long, untidy mane.

97
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:43,901
Horses like this were tough and sure footed, and could carry a rider or a load along the steep paths of the fjord in any weather.

98
00:11:43,901 --> 00:11:52,538
Tonight, he dozes on his feet, one back hoof tipped up and resting, his lower lip drooping, his eyes half closed.

99
00:11:54,038 --> 00:11:57,550
That hay was the most precious thing on the farm.

100
00:11:57,550 --> 00:12:09,163
All through the short summer, the family had cut grass on the meadows and on the steep slopes above the fjord, and dried it, and carried it home in great bundles.

101
00:12:09,163 --> 00:12:14,395
Every evening now, someone measures out a careful armful for each animal.

102
00:12:14,395 --> 00:12:20,918
The whole question of the winter was whether the hay would last until the grass grew again.

103
00:12:22,418 --> 00:12:29,381
The sheep are kept nearby, in a low turf shed of their own, close against the wall of the house.

104
00:12:29,381 --> 00:12:35,329
They are a hardy, short tailed northern breed, with thick fleeces of many colours.

105
00:12:35,329 --> 00:12:38,593
White, and grey, and brown, and nearly black.

106
00:12:38,593 --> 00:12:47,442
Through the winter they huddle together in the straw, a single warm, woolly mass, breathing slow clouds into the cold air.

107
00:12:48,942 --> 00:12:53,126
A girl comes through the partition with a wooden pail.

108
00:12:53,126 --> 00:13:01,648
She sits on a low stool beside one of the cows, leans her forehead against its warm flank, and begins to milk.

109
00:13:01,648 --> 00:13:10,016
The sound is soft and rhythmic, a thin ringing at first, and then a deeper, gentler sound as the pail fills.

110
00:13:10,016 --> 00:13:12,883
The cow does not seem to mind at all.

111
00:13:12,883 --> 00:13:16,292
She has done this every evening of her life.

112
00:13:17,792 --> 00:13:26,351
Before the household goes to sleep, someone will take a small lamp and walk the length of the byre one more time.

113
00:13:26,351 --> 00:13:33,773
To see that each animal is settled, that the water troughs have not frozen, that nothing is wrong.

114
00:13:33,773 --> 00:13:41,045
It is a quiet, unhurried walk, past the warm, breathing shapes in the dark, and it ends the day.

115
00:13:42,545 --> 00:13:45,948
Some of that milk will be drunk fresh tonight.

116
00:13:45,948 --> 00:13:57,636
Most of it will be turned into butter, and cheese, and skyr, a thick, tangy kind of soured milk that was eaten in the north for centuries, and still is today.

117
00:13:57,636 --> 00:14:05,256
In the cold months, what the animals gave, and what had been stored from the summer, was all there was.

118
00:14:05,256 --> 00:14:07,697
And so nothing at all was wasted.

119
00:14:17,197 --> 00:14:20,899
Back in the main room, supper is being prepared.

120
00:14:20,899 --> 00:14:28,380
Over one end of the long hearth, an iron cauldron hangs from a chain, fixed to a beam high above.

121
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:31,466
Inside it, something is bubbling slowly.

122
00:14:31,466 --> 00:14:40,258
A thick porridge of barley, cooked in water with a little milk, and stirred now and then with a long wooden spoon.

123
00:14:41,758 --> 00:14:46,499
Beside the fire, a flat stone has been heating in the embers.

124
00:14:46,499 --> 00:14:55,826
A woman takes a handful of dough, made from barley flour and water, and presses it out into a thin round with her palms.

125
00:14:55,826 --> 00:14:58,080
She lays it on the hot stone.

126
00:14:58,080 --> 00:15:04,142
It puffs and blisters and browns in only a moment, and then she turns it over.

127
00:15:04,142 --> 00:15:08,340
Flat bread like this was baked fresh almost every day.

128
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:15,559
From the rafters, in the smoky air near the roof, hang the stores of winter.

129
00:15:15,559 --> 00:15:22,107
Strips of meat and sides of mutton, slowly drying and taking on the taste of the smoke.

130
00:15:22,107 --> 00:15:23,386
Bundles of herbs.

131
00:15:23,386 --> 00:15:32,041
And rows of fish, split open and dried hard in the cold wind of early spring, as stiff and light as pieces of wood.

132
00:15:32,041 --> 00:15:38,362
Before they are eaten, they are soaked, and then beaten with a stone to soften them.

133
00:15:39,862 --> 00:15:42,963
Water for the cooking comes from the river.

134
00:15:42,963 --> 00:15:55,365
Earlier, one of the boys walked down with an axe and two wooden buckets, and cut a hole through the ice near the bank, where the water still runs fast and clear underneath.

135
00:15:55,365 --> 00:16:08,705
He carried the buckets back up the path, slowly, steam rising from the water in the cold air, and set them down by the hearth, where the thin skin of ice on top has already melted away.

136
00:16:10,205 --> 00:16:14,387
Remember the name of the month that comes after this one.

137
00:16:14,387 --> 00:16:18,717
Mörsugur, which means something like the fat sucking month.

138
00:16:18,717 --> 00:16:25,028
By then, the fresh food of autumn was gone, and people lived on what they had laid by.

139
00:16:25,028 --> 00:16:30,017
Butter and lard, and dried meat, and the rich marrow from the bones.

140
00:16:30,017 --> 00:16:37,576
It was a name that made people smile, the way we might joke about the long, lean weeks after a holiday.

141
00:16:39,076 --> 00:16:42,182
Now the family gathers along the benches.

142
00:16:42,182 --> 00:16:52,184
Everyone has their own wooden bowl, and their own spoon, carved from wood or from horn, and their own small knife, worn at the belt.

143
00:16:52,184 --> 00:16:54,988
The porridge is ladled out, steaming.

144
00:16:54,988 --> 00:17:01,429
There is bread, and butter, and a little dried fish, and a lump of soft white cheese.

145
00:17:01,429 --> 00:17:04,612
For a while, nobody says very much at all.

146
00:17:06,112 --> 00:17:12,342
To drink, there is ale, brewed on the farm from barley, and served in wooden cups.

147
00:17:12,342 --> 00:17:16,520
It is mild and a little cloudy, with a faint sweetness.

148
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:27,765
On a special evening, there might be mead, made from honey, but honey was rare this far north, and mead was kept for feasts and for honoured guests.

149
00:17:29,265 --> 00:17:31,376
The bowls are scraped clean.

150
00:17:31,376 --> 00:17:37,936
The fire is fed a few more pieces of birch, and the bark curls and crackles and flares.

151
00:17:37,936 --> 00:17:46,305
Outside, the wind has risen a little, and you can hear it moving over the roof, a long, low sigh, like the sea.

152
00:17:46,305 --> 00:17:48,793
But in here, it cannot reach you.

153
00:17:48,793 --> 00:17:54,524
In here, there is only the fire, and the warmth, and the slow end of a meal.

154
00:17:56,024 --> 00:18:03,561
The dog, who has been lying very patiently beside the hearth through the whole meal, is given a bone.

155
00:18:03,561 --> 00:18:15,501
He carries it off to a corner under the bench, and you can hear him there in the shadows for a long time afterward, gnawing contentedly, and then falling quiet.

156
00:18:25,001 --> 00:18:27,646
After supper, nobody goes to bed.

157
00:18:27,646 --> 00:18:33,017
The winter evening is long, and there is always work for the hands.

158
00:18:33,017 --> 00:18:41,193
But it is a quiet, sitting kind of work, the kind that lets the mind wander, and leaves room for talk.

159
00:18:42,693 --> 00:18:46,166
Near the lamps, one of the women is spinning.

160
00:18:46,166 --> 00:18:52,648
In her left hand she holds a staff with a soft cloud of combed wool tied to the top.

161
00:18:52,648 --> 00:19:03,530
With her right hand she draws out a few fibres and twists them, and lets a little wooden spindle drop toward the floor, spinning as it falls.

162
00:19:03,530 --> 00:19:12,868
The spindle has a round weight near its base, called a whorl, made of soapstone or clay, which keeps it turning smoothly.

163
00:19:12,868 --> 00:19:20,276
The thread grows longer, and longer, and is wound onto the spindle, and the spindle drops again.

164
00:19:21,776 --> 00:19:26,608
Every thread that the household wore was made this way, by hand.

165
00:19:26,608 --> 00:19:30,382
Every shirt, every dress, every cloak and blanket.

166
00:19:30,382 --> 00:19:32,270
And above all, the sails.

167
00:19:32,270 --> 00:19:42,461
A single large sail for a sea going ship took an enormous amount of wool, and many months, even years, of patient spinning and weaving.

168
00:19:42,461 --> 00:19:48,123
Without this quiet work by the fire, there would have been no ships at all.

169
00:19:49,623 --> 00:19:51,979
Against the wall stands the loom.

170
00:19:51,979 --> 00:20:00,688
It is tall and upright, leaning back slightly against the wall, and the threads hang straight down from a beam at the top.

171
00:20:00,688 --> 00:20:09,326
At the bottom, they are held tight by rows of heavy weights, made of baked clay, each one shaped like a small, flat ring.

172
00:20:09,326 --> 00:20:18,892
Archaeologists find these loom weights in great numbers, often lying in a neat row on the floor, exactly where an old loom once stood.

173
00:20:20,392 --> 00:20:22,527
The weaver works standing up.

174
00:20:22,527 --> 00:20:35,925
She passes the thread back and forth through the hanging warp, and then beats it upward with a long, smooth sword of wood or whalebone, so that the cloth grows from the top downward.

175
00:20:35,925 --> 00:20:38,722
Each pass makes a soft, muffled knock.

176
00:20:38,722 --> 00:20:44,243
It is a slow, steady rhythm, and it fills the house like a quiet heartbeat.

177
00:20:45,743 --> 00:20:48,425
On the other bench, a man is carving.

178
00:20:48,425 --> 00:20:57,270
He holds a block of birch wood in one hand and a small knife in the other, and long, pale curls of wood fall into his lap.

179
00:20:57,270 --> 00:20:59,083
He is making a new spoon.

180
00:20:59,083 --> 00:21:12,495
When it is finished, he will carve a little pattern into the handle, two creatures twisting around each other, their bodies looping and interlacing, in the style the Norse people loved.

181
00:21:13,995 --> 00:21:17,212
Some of the wool on the benches has been dyed.

182
00:21:17,212 --> 00:21:20,850
A soft blue, from the leaves of a plant called woad.

183
00:21:20,850 --> 00:21:26,446
A warm red, from the roots of madder, which had to be traded from further south.

184
00:21:26,446 --> 00:21:29,943
Yellows and greens from the plants of the meadows.

185
00:21:29,943 --> 00:21:36,518
Most everyday clothing was left in the natural colours of the sheep, white and grey and brown.

186
00:21:36,518 --> 00:21:44,352
But a little bright colour, at the edge of a cloak or around a sleeve, was a quiet pleasure, and a sign of care.

187
00:21:45,852 --> 00:21:50,967
An older woman is making a sock, but not in the way you might expect.

188
00:21:50,967 --> 00:22:03,345
She uses a single flat needle, carved from bone, with an eye at one end, and she builds the fabric up in small loops, one through another, using short lengths of yarn.

189
00:22:03,345 --> 00:22:08,089
This method is called nålbinding, and it is older than knitting.

190
00:22:08,089 --> 00:22:13,500
The fabric it makes is thick, and warm, and almost impossible to unravel.

191
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:18,628
For a long time, there is no sound but the work.

192
00:22:18,628 --> 00:22:20,517
The click of the spindle.

193
00:22:20,517 --> 00:22:22,180
The knock of the loom.

194
00:22:22,180 --> 00:22:25,354
The whisper of the knife through the wood.

195
00:22:25,354 --> 00:22:29,208
And the fire, murmuring to itself along the hearth.

196
00:22:38,708 --> 00:22:45,159
Close to the fire, two of the young men have pulled a board between them on the bench.

197
00:22:45,159 --> 00:22:55,285
It is a square of wood, cut with a grid of small squares, and on it stand two little armies of playing pieces, some pale and some dark.

198
00:22:55,285 --> 00:23:02,260
They are playing hnefatafl, the king's table, one of the favourite games of the Viking world.

199
00:23:03,760 --> 00:23:08,161
It is an unusual game, because the two sides are not equal.

200
00:23:08,161 --> 00:23:15,544
One player has a king, standing on the very centre square, surrounded by a small band of defenders.

201
00:23:15,544 --> 00:23:21,138
The other player has a larger army, arranged around the edges of the board.

202
00:23:21,138 --> 00:23:28,074
The defenders are trying to lead their king safely to the edge, or into a corner, and escape.

203
00:23:28,074 --> 00:23:32,326
The attackers are trying to surround him and capture him.

204
00:23:33,825 --> 00:23:44,065
Every piece moves the same way, in a straight line, as far as it likes, along the rows of the board, much like the rook in chess.

205
00:23:44,065 --> 00:23:48,906
A piece is taken when it is trapped between two enemy pieces.

206
00:23:48,906 --> 00:23:52,955
And so the game is all about patience and position.

207
00:23:52,955 --> 00:23:58,828
A single careless move can open a path for the king, or close one forever.

208
00:24:00,328 --> 00:24:07,494
Nobody today knows exactly how the Vikings played it, because they never wrote the rules down.

209
00:24:07,494 --> 00:24:12,450
The rules most people use now come from a much later description.

210
00:24:12,450 --> 00:24:25,867
In the year 1732, the Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus, travelling in the far north, watched the Sámi people playing a similar game called tablut, and wrote down how it worked.

211
00:24:25,867 --> 00:24:30,899
From his notes, the old game has been gently pieced back together.

212
00:24:32,399 --> 00:24:36,109
But the pieces themselves survive, in many places.

213
00:24:36,109 --> 00:24:42,119
Little rounded or cone shaped pieces made of glass, or bone, or antler, or amber.

214
00:24:42,119 --> 00:24:46,793
A set of glass pieces was found in a grave at Birka, in Sweden.

215
00:24:46,793 --> 00:24:55,919
A board for the game was found in the great Gokstad ship burial, in Norway, with a different game marked on its other side.

216
00:24:55,919 --> 00:25:02,671
And people seem to have been buried with their pieces, as if they hoped to keep on playing.

217
00:25:04,171 --> 00:25:06,592
The two players are in no hurry.

218
00:25:06,592 --> 00:25:16,048
One of them leans back against a post, considering the board for a long time, and then moves a single dark piece, one square.

219
00:25:16,048 --> 00:25:21,419
The other nods slowly, and rubs his chin, and looks at the board again.

220
00:25:21,419 --> 00:25:28,832
Their younger sister sits beside them, watching every move, already sure that she could do better.

221
00:25:30,332 --> 00:25:34,765
At the other end of the bench, the grandmother is asking riddles.

222
00:25:34,765 --> 00:25:44,722
The Norse loved these too, and in one of the old sagas, a king and a mysterious guest spend a whole evening trying to outwit each other with them.

223
00:25:44,722 --> 00:25:47,518
The children frown and whisper and guess.

224
00:25:47,518 --> 00:25:52,769
What can fill a whole house from wall to wall, and yet weighs nothing at all?

225
00:25:52,769 --> 00:25:56,111
What runs all night without ever leaving its bed?

226
00:25:56,111 --> 00:26:00,135
The answers are passed around, laughed at, and argued over.

227
00:26:01,635 --> 00:26:04,538
The game will not be finished tonight.

228
00:26:04,538 --> 00:26:06,142
That does not matter.

229
00:26:06,142 --> 00:26:18,595
The board will be set carefully on a shelf, with the pieces left exactly where they are, ready to be taken down again tomorrow evening, and the evening after that.

230
00:26:18,595 --> 00:26:21,880
There are many long evenings still to come.

231
00:26:31,380 --> 00:26:36,825
As the night deepens, the household draws closer around the fire.

232
00:26:36,825 --> 00:26:41,014
The spindle is put away, and the loom falls quiet.

233
00:26:41,014 --> 00:26:47,380
And someone, usually the oldest person in the house, begins to tell a story.

234
00:26:48,880 --> 00:26:52,996
The Norse people loved stories more than almost anything.

235
00:26:52,996 --> 00:26:57,112
Through the long winters, they told them again and again.

236
00:26:57,112 --> 00:27:06,211
Stories of families and their quarrels, of voyages to distant islands, of the gods and the giants and the making of the world.

237
00:27:06,211 --> 00:27:10,977
For centuries, most of these tales were never written down at all.

238
00:27:10,977 --> 00:27:18,704
They lived only in memory, passed from one voice to the next, beside thousands of fires just like this one.

239
00:27:20,204 --> 00:27:31,668
Many of them were finally written down in Iceland, in the twelve hundreds and the thirteen hundreds, by people who wanted to keep the old stories safe.

240
00:27:31,668 --> 00:27:40,398
One of those writers, Snorri Sturluson, gathered up the old tales of the gods in a book we now call the Prose Edda.

241
00:27:40,398 --> 00:27:44,801
And in it, there is a gentle little story about the night.

242
00:27:46,301 --> 00:27:49,471
Snorri also wrote about the way poets spoke.

243
00:27:49,471 --> 00:27:54,010
A Norse poet, a skald, rarely called a thing by its plain name.

244
00:27:54,010 --> 00:27:58,188
Instead, he used little riddling phrases, called kennings.

245
00:27:58,188 --> 00:28:01,357
A ship might be called the horse of the sea.

246
00:28:01,357 --> 00:28:04,743
The sea itself might be the road of the whales.

247
00:28:04,743 --> 00:28:16,701
And gold, Snorri explains, could be called the fire of the sea, because in one old story, the hall of the sea giant was lit not by flames, but by the shining of gold.

248
00:28:18,201 --> 00:28:25,665
The story goes that the night is a woman named Nótt, dark haired and dark eyed, like all her family.

249
00:28:25,665 --> 00:28:32,457
Every evening, she rides across the sky on a horse called Hrímfaxi, which means frost mane.

250
00:28:32,457 --> 00:28:44,697
And every morning, as the horse finishes its long journey, the foam from its bit falls down onto the earth below, and that is the dew you find on the grass at dawn.

251
00:28:46,198 --> 00:28:57,369
Her son is Day, bright and fair, and he rides a horse called Skinfaxi, shining mane, whose mane lights up the whole sky and the earth beneath it.

252
00:28:57,369 --> 00:29:09,003
And so, in the old story, night and day simply take turns, riding one after the other around the world, each in their own time, without hurry, forever.

253
00:29:10,503 --> 00:29:15,046
Outside, far above the fjord, something is happening in the sky.

254
00:29:15,046 --> 00:29:22,854
A faint green glow has appeared over the mountains, like a curtain of thin silk, slowly folding and unfolding.

255
00:29:22,854 --> 00:29:24,273
The northern lights.

256
00:29:24,273 --> 00:29:41,735
A Norwegian book written in the twelve hundreds, called the King's Mirror, describes them as they were seen in Greenland, shining like a great flame, and shooting up long, bright rays, and moving and changing, always shifting from place to place.

257
00:29:43,235 --> 00:29:53,097
The light ripples silently across the stars, green and faintly violet, and then fades a little, and then brightens again.

258
00:29:53,097 --> 00:29:55,134
It makes no sound at all.

259
00:29:55,134 --> 00:29:59,291
The snow on the mountains glows faintly beneath it.

260
00:29:59,291 --> 00:30:05,485
And below, the fjord holds a dim reflection of it on its still, black water.

261
00:30:06,985 --> 00:30:10,436
Inside, the story has turned to the coming feast.

262
00:30:10,436 --> 00:30:10,789
Yule.

263
00:30:10,789 --> 00:30:16,916
In a few weeks, at the darkest point of the year, there will be a great gathering here.

264
00:30:16,916 --> 00:30:27,902
There will be food and ale for days, and neighbours coming in from the other farms along the fjord, and promises made and toasts drunk to the returning sun.

265
00:30:27,902 --> 00:30:33,114
Because after Yule, every day will be a little longer than the one before.

266
00:30:33,114 --> 00:30:35,508
The light will begin to come back.

267
00:30:45,008 --> 00:30:46,646
The story has ended.

268
00:30:46,646 --> 00:30:57,131
The children are already asleep, curled together on the bench under a heavy woollen blanket, their faces lit softly by the fire.

269
00:30:57,131 --> 00:31:04,912
One by one, the grown ups lie down too, spreading out the furs, and pulling the blankets close.

270
00:31:06,412 --> 00:31:09,162
Someone banks the fire for the night.

271
00:31:09,162 --> 00:31:17,486
They rake the glowing embers together, toward the middle of the long hearth, and cover them with a layer of ash.

272
00:31:17,486 --> 00:31:29,452
Under that soft grey blanket, the embers will keep their heat until morning, and with a breath and a handful of dry birch bark, the fire will wake again at dawn.

273
00:31:30,952 --> 00:31:36,397
The lamps are put out, one after another, with a pinch of the fingers.

274
00:31:36,397 --> 00:31:38,498
Now the house is very dark.

275
00:31:38,498 --> 00:31:48,766
Only a dull red glow shows through the ash on the hearth, and the faintest grey light comes down through the smoke hole in the roof.

276
00:31:50,265 --> 00:31:59,135
From the byre at the far end, there comes a long, slow, contented sigh, as one of the cows lies down in the straw.

277
00:31:59,135 --> 00:32:03,803
The sheep in their shed are a single, sleeping heap of wool.

278
00:32:03,803 --> 00:32:12,751
The farm dog has found the warmest place, right beside the hearth, and is lying with his nose tucked into his tail.

279
00:32:14,251 --> 00:32:16,998
Outside, it has begun to snow again.

280
00:32:16,998 --> 00:32:29,969
Soft, large flakes are falling straight down through the windless air, settling on the turf roof, on the overturned boat by the shore, on the long path up from the water.

281
00:32:29,969 --> 00:32:39,659
They fill in the footprints, one by one, until the snow is smooth and new again, as though no one had ever walked there at all.

282
00:32:41,159 --> 00:32:44,897
Down at the shore, the fjord is black and still.

283
00:32:44,897 --> 00:32:53,231
A thin rim of new ice has begun to form along the edge of the water, between the stones, delicate as glass.

284
00:32:53,231 --> 00:33:05,927
Above the mountains, the northern lights have faded to a faint green haze, and then to nothing, and the stars have come back, one by one, through the falling snow.

285
00:33:07,427 --> 00:33:20,075
In the storehouse, the barrels and baskets sit in the cold dark, full of grain and dried fish and butter, enough, if it is used with care, to last until the spring.

286
00:33:20,075 --> 00:33:22,774
In the workshop, the forge is cold.

287
00:33:22,774 --> 00:33:32,877
In the boathouse, the long ship rests on its rollers, its oars stacked along the wall, dreaming, perhaps, of summer and open water.

288
00:33:34,377 --> 00:33:45,289
The house sits quietly at the head of the fjord, a long white shape in the white snow, with a thin thread of smoke still rising from its roof.

289
00:33:45,289 --> 00:33:50,667
Inside, the whole household breathes slowly together in the warm dark.

290
00:33:50,667 --> 00:33:54,970
The people, and the animals, and the fire under its ash.

291
00:33:54,970 --> 00:33:59,273
All of them waiting, patiently, for the light to return.

292
00:34:00,773 --> 00:34:02,546
You can wait with them.

293
00:34:02,546 --> 00:34:05,319
There is nothing more to do tonight.

294
00:34:05,319 --> 00:34:07,323
Let the snow keep falling.

295
00:34:07,323 --> 00:34:09,865
Let the embers keep their warmth.

296
00:34:09,865 --> 00:34:16,645
Let the long winter night hold you, the way it held that small, quiet house so long ago.

297
00:34:18,145 --> 00:34:23,590
Thank you for spending this winter evening with The Quiet Archive.

298
00:34:23,590 --> 00:34:24,662
Sleep warmly.

299
00:34:24,662 --> 00:34:25,652
Rest deeply.

300
00:34:25,652 --> 00:34:26,807
And goodnight.
